data.content:
The lightest approach depends on your target framework:

### .NET 8+ → Use the built-in `TimeProvider`

No new abstractions needed. Accept `TimeProvider` as a parameter with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (one NuGet package):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, ...)));
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(1)); // controllable
```

Production callers don't change — `new InvoiceCalculator()` keeps using real time.

### Pre-.NET 8 → Thin `IClock` interface

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }
public class SystemClock : IClock {
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Same constructor-default pattern, tests pass a stub.

---

**Recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, `TimeProvider` is the way — zero custom abstractions, one test-only NuGet package, and the optional parameter means no DI container required.

